SFCLoad: A simple SNES ROM loader for SD2SNES/FXPak Pro flash cartridges
------------------------------------------------------------------------

This tool is used to instantly upload and start ROMs on an SNES/Super Famicom
console, using any USB-enabled SD2SNES flash carts.


Why this was made
=================

I made this program as a quick and dirty solution to instantly upload ROMs to
my FXPak Pro flashcart via the command line, without the use of graphical tools
or WebSocket servers, which is a bit more than I need for my own personal use-
case. I was also curious as to see how the usb2snes protocol worked under the
hood (using a mix of Wireshark/USBPcap sniffing and inspecting the firmware code
for the sd2snes USB interface), and thought this would be a fun little project
to try out.

Do note that this program is very basic and minimal by design. If you need 
something a bit more powerful, such as reading/writing to memory or using with 
emulators, I can highly recommend you check out QUsb2snes by Skarsnik, which
can be found here: https://skarsnik.github.io/QUsb2snes/

I would also like to give some thanks and shout-outs to the sd2snes project,
as well as the original usb2snes firmware project:
https://sd2snes.de
https://usb2snes.com/


Requirements
============

Your flashcart (either an SD2SNES or FXPak Pro) must be running a firmware that
supports USB communication. The sd2snes firmware version 1.11.0 and newer all
supports USB (thanks to RedGuyy's original work on ubs2snes). It is highly  
recommended to use the latest version of the firmware: http://sd2snes.de/


Basic usage
===========

First, make sure your flash cart is connected to your computer with a USB cable,
and that the game console is powered on. The flash cart should show up on your
computer as a serial device. Example would be "/dev/ttyACM0" on Linux, or "COM3"
on Windows. Please note that the serial device's name is different between
systems. Thankfully, if you do not know the name of the device, SFCLoad is able
to automatically scan and detect the device for you.

The most simple way to run SFCLoad is to give it a ROM file, like so:

    $ sfcload SuperAwesomeGame.sfc

Here, the program will automatically scan for a compatible flash cart, and once
a device is found, it will upload the ROM to the device. Once the upload is 
finished, it will ask if you would like to boot up the ROM. If you choose "Yes"
(Y), the program on the console will immediately start.

A folder named "SFCLoad ROMs" is created in your flash cart's storage. This is
where all ROMs uploaded using SFCLoad will be stored by default.

SFCLoad uses a special naming scheme when uploading ROMs to the flash cart. It
usually looks something like this:

    rom_SUPER-AWESOME-GAME_cbf43926.sfc

The file name is in three parts (excluding the extension): a `rom` prefix,
followed by the program title (extracted from the header of the ROM), followed
by a CRC32 checksum of the ROM. This naming schemes ensures that no duplicates
will be stored on the flash card (saving space), while still uploading new or
modified versions of the ROM if the checksum changes. This is especially useful
for homebrew developers, so they can have different ROM revisions stored without
any duplicates of identical ROMs.

Of course, you can also keep the original file name by passing the `-N` option.
Although be aware that it might overwrite existing files if they share the same
name.

For more information on the other available options, please refer to the usage
text inside the program by passing the `-h` option.


Troubleshooting and known issues
================================

On Linux and BSD, you may have to run the program with elevated privileges, in
order to access serial devices. This can be done by using `sudo` on Linux or
`doas` on BSD. On Windows and macOS, it *shouldn't* be necessary to run with 
elevated privileges, but your mileage may vary.

When uploading a ROM to the flash cart, it is strongly recommended *not* to
abort the process (i.e.: sending a SIGTERM signal to the program, or killing the
process), as this will put the flash cart's USB interface in a confused 
state, where it's unable to process any further communications. This can also
happen if the program gets re-run too quickly while the device is busy, but it's
rare, at least from my own observations.

If the flash cart doesn't seem to be accepting any further commands, the most 
common fix is to just power off the console for a second, and then power it back
on again, which should put the flash cart's USB interface in a clean state.


Building from source
====================

This program requires libserialport to build, which is available in most package
managers, or can be downloaded here: https://sigrok.org/wiki/Libserialport

This program uses GNU Autotools to build.

First things first, if you've downloaded this straight from the git repository,
and not from a tarball, then please run `./autogen.sh` first.

    $ ./autogen.sh

To build, run the following in your terminal:

    $ mkdir build && cd build
    $ ../configure
    $ make

Note: If you've downloaded the source straight from the git repository, you may
have to run `./autogen.sh` *before* you run the other commands above!

Afterwards, to install, run `make install` (might need elevated privileges).


Building on Windows
===================

If you're on Windows, you will need to use mingw-w64, preferably with MSYS2:
https://www.mingw-w64.org/

Please refer to MSYS2's documentation on how to install the required tools, such
as autotools and libserialport. After that's done, you should be able to build 
the program using the same commands above.

WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux) works as well, however, you will need to use
something like usbipd-win to expose the USB device to the guest. Please refer 
to this guide: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/connect-usb


